<<The material library (macOS)>>

@ In metal-cpp every Metal object is reference counted, as every Direct3D object is, and
|NS::SharedPtr| plays the part of |ComPtr|: it releases its object when it is destroyed.
There is one difference in the vocabulary. Direct3D separates a texture (memory) from its
\emph{views} (interpretations of that memory, the ``shader resource views''); Metal has
only textures, but a texture can be created as a \emph{view} of another one with a
different pixel format. So |texLinear| is the texture itself and |texSrgb| a view of it.

<<Map images (macOS)>>=
// One texture: kept both on the GPU (for rendering / UI) and on the CPU (for the inspector).
struct MapImage {
    int width = 0, height = 0;
    std::vector<uint8_t> rgba;                    // CPU copy, 8 bits per channel
    NS::SharedPtr<MTL::Texture> texLinear;        // raw values (data maps, UI display)
    NS::SharedPtr<MTL::Texture> texSrgb;          // a view decoding sRGB (color, for shading)

    // Bilinear sample with wrap addressing; channels in [0,1].
    simd::float4 Sample(float u, float v) const;
    // Nearest texel fetch with wrap addressing (red channel).
    float Texel(int x, int y) const;
};

@ Direct3D~11 has a \emph{device}, which creates objects, and an \emph{immediate context},
which executes commands at once. Metal has a device too, but commands are recorded into
\emph{command buffers} that are taken from a \emph{command queue} and then submitted. The
library therefore receives the queue where the Windows version receives the context.

<<The material library (macOS)>>=
class MaterialLibrary {
public:
    bool Load(const std::filesystem::path& materialsDir, std::string& error);
    // Lazily uploads a material's textures the first time it is selected.
    void EnsureLoaded(int index, MTL::Device* device, MTL::CommandQueue* queue);
    std::vector<Material>& Items() { return items_; }

private:
    std::filesystem::path dir_;
    std::vector<Material> items_;
};

@ The implementation reuses the decoder, the names, texel access and the parsing of the
manifest. Bilinear sampling differs only in the type of its result: the interpolation
itself is the module of the first part. This matters, since the inspector's numbers must
be the same on both systems.

